---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Wealth screening & prospect research software for nonprofits"
  description="DonorSearch, Kindsight (iWave), and WealthEngine compared — what wealth screening costs, what it actually tells you, and the shop size where it starts paying off."
  section="prospect-research">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Wealth prospecting' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Wealth screening &amp; prospect research</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Wealth screening scores your donors on capacity — assets, real estate,
        stock, past giving — so gift officers spend cultivation time on the right
        people. It's the most expensive category in the stack (real contracts
        start around $4,000–$5,000 a year) and the easiest to buy too early.
      </p>

      <h2>The honest economics</h2>
      <p>
        None of the major vendors publish prices; every deal is quoted. Based on
        widely reported figures, entry contracts run roughly
        <strong>$4,150/yr for Kindsight (formerly iWave)</strong>, around
        <strong>$4,000–$6,000/yr for DonorSearch</strong>, and about
        <strong>$5,000/yr for WealthEngine</strong> — all scaling with seats and
        screening volume. Treat those as negotiating anchors, not list prices,
        and get every number in writing.
      </p>

      <h2>When it pays off</h2>
      <p>
        The rule that holds across hundreds of shops: screening earns its cost
        when there's a major-gifts function to act on it — typically around $1M
        raised, but the trigger is a person with a portfolio, not a revenue line.
        A screen that lands in a drawer is the most expensive PDF in fundraising.
        Below the threshold, hand-research your top 25 prospects instead; when
        that stops keeping up, that's your buy signal.
      </p>
      <p>
        And remember what the data is: capacity is not intent. A high score means
        someone <em>could</em> give — the cultivation still has to discover
        whether they <em>will</em>. Platforms that weight demonstrated
        philanthropy (DonorSearch's specialty) get you closer to intent than raw
        wealth markers do.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="prospect-research" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Screening enters the recommended stack at
        <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">$1M–$5M</a> and becomes continuous at
        <a href="/stacks/5m-plus/">$5M+</a>.
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
